WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.629
OK, let's get into audience questions for
the week, some very good ones.

00:00:03.629 --> 00:00:05.340
First one is about Trump voters.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:12.290
Hey, David, why is it that rural and blue
collar as well as older white people seem

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:14.020
to favor Donald Trump?

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:15.450
So, yes, that is true.

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:20.340
It's important to remember that increasingly
this group is not leaning as much towards

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:21.940
Trump as they as they once were.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:28.360
We looked at numbers in I believe it was Pennsylvania,
maybe Wisconsin, some other places where the

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:34.361
white vote is not as in favor of Donald Trump
this year as it was in 2016.

00:00:34.361 --> 00:00:36.370
But the final numbers remain to be seen.

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:43.850
But, yes, it's true that Donald Trump's strongest
demographic group remains white folks and

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:48.480
older folks, although older folks overall
have abandoned Trump and now favor Joe Biden.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.239
But the question is, why is a good one?

00:00:50.239 --> 00:00:56.860
And understanding the why is necessary if
Democrats don't want to make the same mistakes

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:58.829
as as they did in 2016?

00:00:58.829 --> 00:01:05.059
Now, of course, 2016 was rife with, you know,
James, his press conferences about Hillary

00:01:05.059 --> 00:01:10.000
Clinton's emails and foreign meddling, you
know, all these different factors.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.430
But there was a bit of a lack of understanding
from from Hillary Clinton and Democrats about

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:17.389
how to talk about economic anxiety.

00:01:17.389 --> 00:01:18.600
Now, there was racism.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:24.460
There's this big debate over anybody saying
economic anxiety explains Trump's win is ignoring

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:25.460
racism.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:26.460
No, it was both.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:29.819
And for some people, it was more one more,
more one or the other.

00:01:29.819 --> 00:01:35.109
But there's no doubt that over the last 20,
30 years of globalization, as we've seen much

00:01:35.109 --> 00:01:40.939
manufacturing go elsewhere and the U.S. become
more of a service based economy, the people

00:01:40.939 --> 00:01:47.310
disproportionately affected by that are folks
working in manufacturing jobs.

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:51.259
Manufacturing jobs are disproportionately
in more rural areas and Midwestern states

00:01:51.259 --> 00:01:53.450
and in other places like that.

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:59.569
And those who have benefited from the United
States moving towards being a service economy

00:01:59.569 --> 00:02:05.759
have been people on the coasts, in cities,
tech workers, service workers and others.

00:02:05.759 --> 00:02:15.120
So one reality is that on in total, you know,
there was this neo liberal idea that in the

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:19.090
aggregate, the U.S. would do better.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:21.150
The economy would in total be better.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:26.610
There would be more total wealth and lower
overall prices with this change of moving

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:32.290
manufacturing out and creating service and
information jobs that happened.

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:37.549
But it was overwhelmingly concentrated in
certain parts of the country and not in others.

00:02:37.549 --> 00:02:42.960
But politics aside, it was also overwhelmingly
concentrated for the one percent as we see

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:50.439
stagnant wages for large swaths of Americans
and huge income and wealth gains for the top

00:02:50.439 --> 00:02:53.890
one percent and even groups smaller than the
top one percent.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:03:02.730
So for some people, they purt fell for Trump's
rhetoric around trade and the middle class.

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:05.530
It was B.S. populist rhetoric.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:07.120
It went nowhere.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:11.709
But there was a group already predisposed
to favor Republicans in many parts of the

00:03:11.709 --> 00:03:17.129
country, rural, older, white, who fell for
it.

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:21.230
And it has not gone particularly well for
them now separately.

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:28.230
If you look at evangelical and religious voters,
they also are predisposed to vote on abortion

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:29.230
lines.

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:31.580
Trump claimed to be anti-abortion in 2016.

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:35.420
He still claims to Hillary Clinton was in
favor of a woman's right to choose.

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:37.999
So for some folks, it's not actually about
economics.

00:03:37.999 --> 00:03:43.530
It's as simple as as abortion, which continues
to be a not only a voting issue, but the voting

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:44.870
issue for many Americans.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:49.380
So there's whenever we want to look into these
questions, this is part of what I spoke about

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:56.930
earlier this week in our interview about the
U.S. possibly as several countries, 331 million

00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:57.930
people.

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:00.379
Most countries don't have this many people.

00:04:00.379 --> 00:04:06.480
And you have radically different not just
ideologies, but concerns.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.180
And day to day life, the day to day life of
someone who lives in Boston and works at a

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:16.539
tech firm has almost nothing to do with the
life of someone who is a, you know, rural

00:04:16.539 --> 00:04:18.680
Ohio factory worker.

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:20.079
We speak the same language.

00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:21.519
We're Americans, et cetera.

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:26.720
But these are very live different day to day
experience, both culturally and even just

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:29.449
in the nuts and bolts logistics of it.

00:04:29.449 --> 00:04:35.460
And you can't really give one answer to any
question that asks about such a broad trend

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:36.729
in a country of 330 million people.

